# Sprint Cycle 0 & 1 Meeting Notes

## Sprint Cycle 0 Meeting 1
* **Date:** September 2026
* **Meeting Type:** Team Planning Meeting
* **Purpose:** Introduce the project and establish the team's initial plan.


### Discussion
* Reviewed the overall purpose of the course project.
* Discussed the application idea and the problem the system is intended to address.
* Reviewed the initial project requirements provided by the instructor.
* Confirmed team members and discussed each member's responsibilities.
* Discussed communication methods and how the team would coordinate work.
* Discussed the importance of keeping project files and documentation organized.

### Decisions
* Continue developing the proposed student resource application.
* Use GitHub for project source code and collaboration.
* Communicate regularly as a team and document important meetings.
* Divide project responsibilities among team members.

### Action Items
- [ ] Confirm the project name and repository.
- [ ] Review the requirements in more detail.
- [ ] Set up the GitHub repository.
- [ ] Begin researching the system's potential users and features.


## Sprint Cycle 0 Meeting 2
* **Date:** September 2026
* **Meeting Type:** Requirements Planning Meeting
* **Purpose:** Review the project's functional requirements and determine the basic structure of the system.


### Discussion
* Reviewed the Functional Requirements document.
* Identified the primary users who would interact with the system.
* Discussed the types of resources and services the application should provide.
* Discussed the main functions that each user role may need.
* Reviewed potential use cases for the application.
* Discussed the information that would need to be stored by the system.
* Considered the technologies that could be used during development.
* Discussed how the requirements would guide future sprint cycles.

### Decisions
* Use the Functional Requirements document as the primary reference for development.
* Organize system functions around the identified user roles.
* Begin with the core functionality before adding additional features.
* Continue refining requirements before moving into detailed UI design.

### Action Items
- [ ] Finalize the list of user roles.
- [ ] Review and clarify the proposed use cases.
- [ ] Continue evaluating the technology stack.
- [ ] Update the project documentation.
- [ ] Prepare requirements information for the next sprint.

---

## Sprint Cycle 0 Meeting 3
* **Date:** September 2026
* **Meeting Type:** Sprint Review and Planning Meeting
* **Purpose:** Review Sprint Cycle 0 progress and prepare for the next sprint.

### Discussion
* Reviewed the work completed during Sprint Cycle 0.
* Confirmed that the team had reviewed the project requirements.
* Reviewed the proposed user roles and major system functions.
* Checked the GitHub repository and confirmed that team members could access it.
* Discussed the project's planned technology stack and development approach.
* Reviewed the tasks that would need to be completed during Sprint Cycle 1.
* Discussed beginning the application's user interface design in the next sprint.
* Identified the screens that would eventually be needed based on the requirements.

### Decisions
* Move forward with the current project concept.
* Use the requirements and use cases from Sprint Cycle 0 as the foundation for Sprint Cycle 1.
* Begin designing the application's interface during Sprint Cycle 1.
* Continue using GitHub for collaboration and version control.

### Action Items
- [ ] Finalize any remaining Sprint Cycle 0 documentation.
- [ ] Prepare the project for UI development.
- [ ] Begin planning the sign-in and navigation structure for Sprint Cycle 1.
- [ ] Assign Sprint Cycle 1 tasks to team members.
- [ ] Continue documenting team meetings and project progress.


## Sprint Cycle 1 Meeting 1
* **Date:** September 2026
* **Meeting Type:** Team Planning Meeting
* **Purpose:** Review the Functional Requirements document and determine the initial application structure.


### Discussion
* Reviewed the Functional Requirements document and discussed the main features of the application.
* Identified the different user roles and what each role should be able to access.
* Discussed the sign-in process and role-based navigation.
* Reviewed the Sprint Cycle 0 deliverables and divided responsibilities among team members.
* Discussed the GitHub repository and confirmed that each team member should have access.
* Agreed that the first priority would be creating the basic UI screens before beginning major application implementation.

### Decisions
* Create a sign-in screen as the starting point of the application.
* Create separate landing/navigation screens based on user roles.
* Select two use cases for each role to represent in the initial UI design.
* Keep the initial screens simple and consistent so they can be modified during later sprint cycles.
* Decided Lloyd will design the sign-in screen, Naser will design the Role-Based Landing/Navigation Screens, Isaiah will design 
screens for two use cases for each role, and Ondrea will implement a simple application splash/home screen.

### Action Items
- [ ] Create the GitHub repository and verify team member access.
- [ ] Begin designing the sign-in screen.
- [ ] Identify the two use cases selected for each role.
- [ ] Review the requirements before the next meeting.

**Next Meeting:** Review initial screen designs and confirm the selected use cases.

# Sprint Cycle 1 Meeting 2

## Meeting 2 - User Interface Design & Sprint Planning

* **Date:** October 2026
* **Meeting Type:** Sprint Planning and UI Design Meeting
* **Purpose:** Begin planning and designing the user interface for Sprint Cycle I.

### Discussion

* Reviewed the Sprint Cycle I requirements for User Interface Design and the Initial Application Screen.
* Discussed the purpose of designing the application's interface before major implementation begins.
* Reviewed the Functional Requirements document to determine the screens needed for the application's different roles and use cases.
* Discussed the required Sign-In Screen and any related screens that may be needed.
* Discussed the role-based landing and navigation screens that will be designed for each system role.
* Reviewed the requirement to select two meaningful use cases for each role and design the screens needed to support them.
* Discussed the importance of including different types of screens, such as search, detail, forms, reports, dashboards, and other appropriate interfaces.
* Discussed using Figma to create the UI mockups.
* Discussed the requirement to implement a simple Splash/Home Screen in the actual application.
* Reviewed the required elements for the Splash/Home Screen, including the application name, logo or placeholder, project description, and navigation placeholders.
* Discussed that the Splash/Home Screen is only a technical proof of the development environment and GitHub workflow and does not require working application functionality.
* Discussed how Sprint Cycle I materials will be organized and stored in the team's GitHub repository.
* Discussed the requirement for each team member to make at least one meaningful contribution to the GitHub repository.
* Discussed the need for each team member to pull the current repository and verify that the application runs in their development environment.

### Decisions

* Use Figma to create the Sign-In Screen and other UI mockups.
* Store the completed UI designs and Sprint Cycle I materials in the team's GitHub repository.
* Implement only the required Splash/Home Screen during this sprint.
* Do not implement authentication, database connections, user roles, or other application functionality during Sprint Cycle I.
* Design the role-based landing pages and screens based on the Functional Requirements.
* Divide the UI design and implementation responsibilities among team members.
* Continue documenting team meetings and important project decisions throughout the sprint.

### Action Items

* [ ] Complete the Sign-In Screen design in Figma.
* [ ] Identify the application's system roles from the Functional Requirements.
* [ ] Design the landing/navigation screen for each system role.
* [ ] Select two use cases for each role.
* [ ] Design the screens needed for the selected use cases.
* [ ] Implement the simple Splash/Home Screen.
* [ ] Add the Splash/Home Screen source code to GitHub.
* [ ] Export completed UI mockups and add them to the GitHub repository.
* [ ] Assign meaningful GitHub contributions to each team member.
* [ ] Have each team member pull the latest repository and verify that the application runs.
* [ ] Begin preparing the Sprint Cycle I presentation.
